WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
Runtime hardening in Kubernetes is layered.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.800
The container image determines much of what the Node.js process contains.

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.060
The Pod security context determines what the process is allowed to do at runtime.

4
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.000
The service account and RBAC rules determine whether the workload can call the Kubernetes API.

5
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.220
Namespace policy and admission controls can enforce baseline expectations.

6
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.880
None of these replaces secure application code, but together they reduce blast radius when something behaves unexpectedly.

7
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:37.600
Most Node.js application Pods should run as non-root where possible and should not require privilege escalation.

8
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:49.540
Security context settings such as runAsNonRoot, allowPrivilegeEscalation, dropped capabilities, seccomp profiles, and read-only root filesystems help make that intent explicit.

9
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.000
There are tradeoffs.

10
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.700
A read-only root filesystem may require an explicit writable directory for temporary files.

11
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.000
A non-root user requires file ownership and permissions to be correct in the image.

12
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.540
Those tradeoffs are manageable when they are designed intentionally rather than discovered during an outage.

13
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.940
Avoid high-risk runtime patterns unless there is a documented and exceptional need.

14
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:24.880
Privileged containers, broad hostPath mounts, Docker socket mounts, host networking, and unnecessary host access can dramatically expand the consequences of a compromised application.

15
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.420
A typical Express-style API should not need those permissions.

16
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:36.440
If a workload asks for them, reviewers should understand exactly why, what safer alternatives were considered, and how the risk is monitored and limited.

17
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.760
Service accounts deserve the same least-privilege thinking.

18
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:44.340
Many Node.js services do not need to call the Kubernetes API at all.

19
00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:50.520
If they do, RBAC should grant only the verbs and resources required for the specific function.

20
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.860
Broad permissions make incident response harder and increase blast radius.

21
00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:04.160
Pod Security Standards and admission policies can help enforce safer defaults, but the application team still needs to understand what permissions their workload actually needs to run.
